.TH ADSSH 1 2026-10-17 "adssh" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
adssh \- programmable DevOps shell that runs POSIX shell and Starlark under one policy
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B adssh
.RI [ options ]
.PP
.B adssh
.RI [ options ]
.B \-c
.I line
.PP
.B adssh
.RI [ options ]
.I script.star
.PP
.B adssh
.RI [ options ]
.I script.adssh
.PP
.B adssh \-\-serve
.I addr
.RI [ options ]
.PP
.B adssh \-\-init
.PP
.B adssh \-\-doctor
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B adssh
is an interactive shell with two languages at one prompt: an embedded POSIX
shell with bash extensions, and Starlark, a small dialect of Python.
Every shell command and every Starlark namespace call is checked by an
OPA/Rego policy and written to an audit log before it runs.
.PP
Each line typed at the prompt is routed to Starlark or to the shell.
The first rule that matches decides:
.IP 1. 4
A line that starts with
.B !
or with
.B $
and a space goes to the shell.
The prefix is removed before the line runs.
.IP 2. 4
A line of the form
.B if
.IR expr :
is Starlark.
A POSIX
.B if
.IR cmd ;
.B then
is shell.
.IP 3. 4
A line that starts with
.BR def ,
.B for
or
.B print(
is Starlark.
.IP 4. 4
A line that starts with
.B [
or
.BR export ,
or has the form
.I NAME=value command
is shell.
.IP 5. 4
A line that contains = and parses as a Starlark assignment is Starlark.
So
.B x = 5
is Starlark and
.B ls \-\-color=auto
is shell.
.IP 6. 4
A line whose first word is a Starlark global or builtin, and which parses as a
Starlark expression, is Starlark.
It stays shell when it is also valid shell and that word is an alias, a
command registered with
.BR sys.register_command ,
a virtual binary, a shell builtin, or a program on PATH.
.IP 7. 4
Everything else is shell.
.PP
A line that starts with a number, such as
.BR "1 + 2" ,
goes to the shell.
Write
.B print(1 + 2)
instead.
.PP
Starlark variables persist for the whole session.
A line that ends in a colon opens a block; the prompt changes to
.B "...   "
and an empty line ends the block and runs it.
.PP
Type
.B help start
at the prompt for a short tour, and
.B help
for the list of topics.
Type
.B exit
or press Ctrl-D on an empty line to leave.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may appear in any order.
An option that is not recognized, or one that needs a value and has none,
prints
.B "adssh: unknown option:"
(or
.BR "option requires an argument:" )
and a usage hint, and exits 2.
Arguments after a script name belong to the script.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.BI \-c " line"
Run
.I line
once and exit.
The line is routed with the same rules as the prompt.
A Starlark expression's value is printed when it is not None.
Profiles are loaded first.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-login
Run as a login shell: source
.B /etc/profile
and
.B ~/.profile
for environment variables, and load
.B /etc/adssh_profile
before the user profiles.
This is also set when argv[0] starts with a dash.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-restricted
Restricted mode.
See SECURITY.
.TP
.BI \-\-policy " path"
Load the Rego policy from
.IR path .
Overrides
.BR ADSSH_POLICY .
.TP
.BI \-\-entitlements " path"
Load RBAC entitlements from the YAML file at
.IR path .
Overrides
.BR ADSSH_ENTITLEMENTS .
.TP
.BI \-\-serve " addr"
Start the SSH server on
.IR addr ,
for example
.BR :2222 .
See SSH SERVER.
.TP
.BI \-\-serve\-agent " addr"
Start the instrumentation-agent HTTP listener on
.IR addr .
It needs
.B ADSSH_AGENT_TOKEN
and runs only the tasks in the allowlist named by
.BR ADSSH_AGENT_TASKS .
Startup fails if either is missing or invalid.
.TP
.B \-\-init
Create
.B ~/.adssh/authorized_keys
and
.B ~/.adssh/policy.rego
and
.BR ~/.adsshrc ,
then exit.
Files that already exist are left alone and listed as skipped.
.TP
.B \-\-doctor
Check the local configuration and print one line per check.
Exits non-zero when a check fails.
.TP
.I script.star
Run the file as Starlark and exit.
A first line starting with
.B #!
is skipped.
.TP
.I script.adssh
Run the file line by line through the same routing, policy and audit path as
the prompt.
A line that starts with
.B \-
may fail without stopping the script.
.TP
.B sah
Speak the sah/1 stdio protocol.
Off unless
.B ADSSH_SAH
is 1, true or yes; when off it prints one error frame and exits.
Enterprise build only; the core build prints an error and exits 2.
.SH STARTUP FILES
At startup adssh runs these Starlark files in this order, into one shared set
of globals:
.TP
.B /etc/adssh_profile
Login shells only.
Skipped when it does not exist.
.TP
.B ~/.adsshprofile
Every session.
Skipped when it does not exist.
.B ADSSH_PROFILE
changes the path.
.TP
.B ~/.adsshrc
Every session.
Created from a short default when it does not exist.
.B ADSSH_RC
changes the path.
.PP
The files are Starlark, not shell.
They can assign
.B PROMPT
and
.BR RPROMPT ,
call
.BR set_keymap ,
call
.B sys.setenv
and
.BR sys.register_command ,
and define the hooks
.B __preexec__
and
.BR __postcmd__ .
An error in one file prints
.B "Error loading profiles"
with the file name and line; the names that file bound before the error are
kept, the files after it still load, and the session starts.
The adssh source tree has a commented example in
.BR examples/adsshrc .
.SH LINE EDITING
The editor uses emacs keys by default.
.B set_keymap("vi")
switches to vi keys and
.B set_keymap("emacs")
switches back, at the prompt or in a startup file.
.PP
Keys in both keymaps:
.TP
.B Enter
Run the line.
.TP
.B Tab
Complete command names, file names, and Starlark names, for example
.B sys.ge
to
.BR sys.getenv .
.TP
.B Ctrl-R
Search history backward.
.B Ctrl-S
searches forward.
.TP
.B "Up and Down arrows"
Previous and next history line.
.TP
.B "Right arrow or Ctrl-F"
At the end of the line, accept the history suggestion.
.TP
.B Ctrl-L
Clear the screen.
.TP
.B Ctrl-C
At the prompt, discard the line.
While a Starlark line runs, stop it.
At the
.B ...
prompt, end the block; what was typed so far still runs.
.TP
.B Ctrl-D
On an empty line, exit.
Otherwise delete the character under the cursor.
.PP
Emacs keys: Ctrl-A start of line, Ctrl-E end of line, Ctrl-B and Ctrl-F one
character back and forward, Alt-B and Alt-F one word back and forward, Ctrl-P
and Ctrl-N previous and next history line, Ctrl-K delete to the end of the
line, Ctrl-U delete to the start, Ctrl-W or Alt-Backspace delete the word
before the cursor, Alt-D delete the word after it, Ctrl-T swap two characters,
Ctrl-Y paste the last deleted text.
.PP
Vi keys: Esc enters command mode, and i, a, I or A return to insert mode.
In command mode h and l move, w, b and e move by word, 0, ^ and $ go to the
start or end of the line, j and k walk history, x deletes a character, dd the
line, dw a word, cw and cc change, r replaces a character, f and t find a
character, p pastes.
.PP
History suggestions show the rest of a matching history line, dimmed, after
the cursor.
.B set suggest on
and
.B set suggest off
turn them on and off.
.B ADSSH_SUGGEST
sets the starting state; the default is on in a local session and off in an
SSH session, where a screen reader would read the dimmed text aloud.
.PP
.B set highlight on
colors dangerous commands before Enter is pressed.
It needs a terminal, the policy must allow the command
.BR "set highlight on" ,
and it is off by default.
.B ADSSH_HIGHLIGHT
turns it on at startup.
.PP
.B set gitprompt on
enables the
.B \eg
git branch escape in
.BR PROMPT .
Type
.B help prompt
for the prompt escapes.
.SH SHELL
Shell lines run in an embedded POSIX shell (mvdan.cc/sh) with bash
extensions.
These work:
.IP \(bu 2
Pipelines:
.B cmd1 | cmd2
.IP \(bu 2
Lists:
.BR "cmd1 && cmd2" ,
.BR "cmd1 || cmd2" ,
.B "cmd1 ; cmd2"
.IP \(bu 2
Redirections:
.BR ">" ,
.BR ">>" ,
.BR "<" ,
.BR "2>" ,
.BR ">&2" ,
.B "2>&1"
.IP \(bu 2
Here-documents
.BR "<< EOF" ,
.B "<<\- EOF"
(leading tabs removed), and here-strings
.B "<<< text"
.IP \(bu 2
Globs
.BR * ,
.B ?
and
.BR [abc] ,
and
.B ~
for the home directory
.IP \(bu 2
Command substitution
.B $(cmd)
and process substitution
.B <(cmd)
.IP \(bu 2
Background
.B cmd &
and
.BR wait .
There is no job table; background jobs are not listed.
.PP
Known limit: file descriptors 3 and above are not supported.
A line with a redirection such as
.BR "3> file" ,
.B "4< file"
or
.B "exec 3> file"
is refused before any of it runs, with
.BR "unsupported redirect fd 3" .
Opening
.B /dev/fd/3
or higher is refused with an error.
.PP
adssh implements some shell verbs itself, including
.BR set ,
.BR alias ,
.BR pushd ,
.BR popd ,
.BR dirs ,
.BR read ,
.BR time ,
.B type
and
.BR trap ,
and ships virtual binaries (built-in commands such as
.BR jq ,
.BR yq ,
.B http
and
.BR audit ).
Type
.B vbins
for the list and
.I name
.B \-\-help
for one of them.
.SH STARLARK
adssh adds these namespaces to the Starlark globals:
sys, str, data, fs, re, crypto, net, log, sec, template, cloud, aws, gcp, az,
oci, k8s, secrets, db, notify, git, github, docker, containers, i18n and
expect.
.PP
A namespace is read-only.
Reach a member with a dot,
.BR sys.getenv ,
or with an index,
.BR sys["getenv"] .
Dicts returned by functions are ordinary dicts and take index access only:
.BR d["key"] .
.PP
Language options AllowSet, AllowGlobalReassign and AllowRecursion are on, so
.B set()
exists, top-level names can be reassigned, while loops and top-level if and
for work, and functions may recurse.
.PP
.B sys.exec_cmd
takes ONE string, not a list.
It runs the string through the adssh shell, so each command in it passes the
policy, and returns standard output and standard error together as one
string.
A command that exits non-zero raises an error with the exit status and the
last 4 KiB of the output.
.B timeout=
is in seconds, default 30.
.B sys.exec_json
does the same and parses the output as JSON; whole numbers become ints and
the rest floats, as in
.BR data.json_parse .
.PP
.B str
is a namespace of string helpers
.RB ( str.split ,
.BR str.upper ,
\&...) and is still the
.B str()
conversion when called:
.B str(5)
is "5".
.PP
.B data.json_dump
and
.B data.yaml_dump
encode a dict (string keys), list, tuple, str, int, float, bool or None;
keys are sorted, so the same value gives the same text.
Any other value, such as a function, is an error.
.PP
.B sys.exec_async
takes one string and returns a job.
.B j.wait()
returns True or an error string;
.B j.output()
waits and returns the output;
.BR j.stop() ,
.B j.resume()
and
.B j.kill()
control it.
.PP
.B sys.parallel
takes a list of command strings and
.BR jobs= ,
runs them together, and returns a list of outputs.
.PP
.BI sys.register_command( name ", " fn )
adds a shell command;
.I fn
receives the argv list, argv[0] being the name.
.PP
The hooks
.BI __preexec__( cmd )
and
.BI __postcmd__( cmd ", " code )
run around every line typed at the interactive prompt.
They can be defined only in a startup file: the prompt refuses to assign any
name that starts with two underscores.
Hooks do not run for
.B \-c
or script files.
.PP
.B load()
is not supported; a line starting with
.B load(
runs as Starlark and fails with "load not implemented by this application".
Use
.B sys.load_plugins
or a startup file.
.SH SECURITY
Every shell command, including each command inside an
.B sys.exec_cmd
string, is checked by the Rego policy, then by the change-management and
four-eyes gates, before it runs.
Every Starlark namespace function call is checked by the same policy as the
command
.BI starlark. namespace . function
with its arguments, for example
.BR starlark.sys.exec_cmd .
A malformed policy or unreadable entitlements file stops startup; a missing
policy file allows everything.
.PP
Restricted mode
.RB ( \-r
or
.BR ADSSH_RESTRICTED )
refuses
.BR cd ,
.BR export ,
command names that contain a slash, and redirection to
.B /dev/tcp
and
.BR /dev/udp .
It leaves out the Starlark file, network and exec functions: sys.read_file,
sys.write_file, sys.exec_cmd, sys.exec_async, sys.exec_json, sys.parallel,
sys.register_command, sys.load_plugin, sys.load_plugins, and all of net.
It refuses plugin loads and fs writes.
It does not block file redirection or assignments such as PATH=/x; use the
policy for those.
.PP
Every command and policy decision is written to the audit log, and each entry
is added to an HMAC hash chain stored next to the log with the suffix
.BR .chain .
.B audit verify
checks the chain.
.SH CORE AND ENTERPRISE BUILDS
adssh is built in two variants from one source tree.
.B go build
gives the core build, under the Apache License 2.0.
.B go build \-tags ee
gives the enterprise build, which adds the files named *_ee.go and the
.B ee/
directory, under the Business Source License 1.1.
.PP
These commands are in the enterprise build only:
4eyes, cm, evidence, elevate, grant, lease, identity, gateway, admin,
grid, flight, agent, worktree, repo, mirror share and mirror unshare,
and the sah subcommand.
In the core build each of these names is kept and prints that it is part of
the enterprise build; it never runs a program of the same name from PATH.
.PP
In the core build,
.B mirror view
and
.B mirror console
attach only to the caller's own sessions.
A command that matches a four-eyes rule in ~/.adssh/foureyes/rules is refused,
because the core build has no way to approve it.
A command covered by
.B ADSSH_CM_PATTERNS
is refused when
.B ADSSH_CM_STRICT
is 1, because the core build cannot accept a change ticket.
If the rules file exists and cannot be read or parsed, every command is
refused, in both builds.
.SH SSH SERVER
.B \-\-serve
.I addr
or
.B ADSSH_SERVE
starts an SSH server that gives each login an adssh session.
Only public-key and certificate logins are accepted.
.PP
Each key line in
.B ~/.adssh/authorized_keys
must name its logins with a quoted
.B user=
option, for example
.BR "user=\(dqalice,bob\(dq ssh-ed25519 AAAA..." .
A key line without
.B user=
stops the server from loading the file.
A line marked
.B cert-authority
trusts a certificate authority; a certificate then logs in only as one of its
principals, and a certificate with no principals is refused.
.B ADSSH_REVOKED_KEYS
names a revocation file.
Root, and any name with uid 0, is refused unless
.B ADSSH_SSH_PERMIT_ROOT
is set.
.PP
Only an interactive shell is offered.
exec requests (ssh host command), sftp, other subsystems, and unknown requests
are refused.
The client's terminal modes from the pty request are applied to the session
terminal, and LANG and LC_* variables sent by the client replace the server's
before the shell starts.
.PP
In the enterprise build the server also authorizes direct-tcpip channels
through gateway policy, so it can serve as a policy-gated jump host.
The core build refuses every direct-tcpip channel.
Without a terminal (for example under systemd) it serves until SIGTERM.
SIGHUP stops new logins and leaves running sessions alone.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Values 1, true and yes turn a switch on unless noted.
.TP
.B ADSSH_RESTRICTED
Restricted mode.
.TP
.B ADSSH_SERVE
SSH server address, such as :2222.
.TP
.B ADSSH_SSH_PERMIT_ROOT
Allow SSH logins as root or any uid-0 name. Default off.
.TP
.B ADSSH_ENTITLEMENTS
RBAC entitlements YAML file.
.TP
.B ADSSH_POLICY
Rego policy file. Default ~/.adssh/policy.rego.
.TP
.B ADSSH_AUDIT_LOG
Audit log. Default ~/.adssh/audit.log.
.TP
.B ADSSH_AUDIT_URL
https webhook for remote audit events. Needs ADSSH_AUDIT_LOG. Private hosts need ADSSH_CALLBACK_ALLOW_PRIVATE.
.TP
.B ADSSH_AUDIT_TOKEN
Bearer token for ADSSH_AUDIT_URL.
.TP
.B ADSSH_HISTORY
Interactive history file. Default ~/.adssh/history.
.TP
.B ADSSH_HOST_KEY
SSH host key. Default ~/.adssh/host_key.
.TP
.B ADSSH_AUTHORIZED_KEYS
SSH authorized_keys. Default ~/.adssh/authorized_keys.
.TP
.B ADSSH_REVOKED_KEYS
SSH revocation file, one SHA256:fingerprint, serial:n or keyid:id per line.
.TP
.B ADSSH_PROFILE
Profile path. Default ~/.adsshprofile.
.TP
.B ADSSH_RC
RC path. Default ~/.adsshrc.
.TP
.B ADSSH_LOG_LEVEL
Listener log level on standard error: error, warn, info or debug. Default info. Any other value stops startup.
.TP
.B ADSSH_LOG_FORMAT
Listener log format: text or json. Default text.
.TP
.B ADSSH_GIT_PROMPT
Show the git branch for \eg in the prompt. Also accepts on. Ignored in restricted mode and SSH sessions.
.TP
.B ADSSH_SUGGEST
Starting state of history suggestions: on, off, 1, 0, true, false, yes or no.
.TP
.B ADSSH_HIGHLIGHT
Turn on danger highlighting at startup, when allowed.
.TP
.B ADSSH_CONTROLPLANE_URL
Control-plane origin for identity device login, https://host[:port]. Unset fails closed. Enterprise build.
.TP
.B ADSSH_OIDC_ISSUER
Issuer for identity oidc import. Unset fails closed. Enterprise build.
.TP
.B ADSSH_OIDC_JWKS_URL
https JWKS URL for that issuer.
.TP
.B ADSSH_OIDC_LOGIN_AUDIENCE
Required audience for identity oidc import. Unset fails closed.
.TP
.B ADSSH_OIDC_ADMIN_ISSUER
Issuer for identity oidc import \-\-provider admin.
.TP
.B ADSSH_OIDC_ADMIN_JWKS_URL
https JWKS URL for the admin provider.
.TP
.B ADSSH_OIDC_ADMIN_AUDIENCE
Required audience for the admin provider. Must differ from the login audience.
.TP
.B ADSSH_CALLBACK_ALLOW_PRIVATE
Allow private address ranges for control-plane, JWKS, webhook and approval URLs. Metadata addresses stay blocked.
.TP
.B ADSSH_AGENT_SERVE
Instrumentation-agent listen address, such as 127.0.0.1:7777.
.TP
.B ADSSH_AGENT_TOKEN
Bearer token the agent requires. Without it the agent does not start.
.TP
.B ADSSH_AGENT_TASKS
Agent task allowlist. Default ~/.adssh/instrument-tasks.yaml.
.TP
.B ADSSH_SAH
Enable the sah subcommand. Only 1, true or yes; anything else stays off. Enterprise build.
.TP
.B ADSSH_GUARD
SSH listener jail: off or on. Unset follows guard.yaml. Any other value fails closed.
.TP
.B ADSSH_GUARD_CONFIG
Jail configuration. Default ~/.adssh/guard.yaml.
.TP
.B ADSSH_GUARD_STATE
Ban file. Default ~/.adssh/guard-state.json.
.TP
.B ADSSH_ADMIN_KEYS
Mode 0600 name=key file for admin serve. A named key can approve, deny and unban as that name.
.TP
.B ADSSH_ADMIN_LISTEN
Enterprise build: start the admin API with the SSH server, on unix:/path or a loopback address.
.TP
.B ADSSH_SNOOP_NOTIFY
Default for the notice shown when support attaches to a session: on or off. Default on.
.TP
.B ADSSH_SNOOP_NOTIFY_LOCK
on forces that notice; no policy rule can silence it.
.TP
.B ADSSH_RECORD_DIR
Session recording directory. Default ~/.adssh/recordings.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
When set, the configuration directory is $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/adssh instead of ~/.adssh.
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
When set, the audit chain key lives in $XDG_DATA_HOME/adssh instead of ~/.adssh.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/adssh_profile
System profile, login shells only.
.TP
.B ~/.adsshprofile
User profile.
.TP
.B ~/.adsshrc
User RC file, created when missing.
.TP
.B ~/.adssh/policy.rego
Default Rego policy.
.TP
.B ~/.adssh/audit.log
Audit log.
.TP
.B ~/.adssh/audit.log.chain
Audit hash chain.
.TP
.B ~/.adssh/audit.key
Audit chain HMAC key.
.TP
.B ~/.adssh/history
Interactive history.
.TP
.B ~/.adssh/host_key
SSH host key.
.TP
.B ~/.adssh/authorized_keys
SSH keys and certificate authorities.
.TP
.B ~/.adssh/guard.yaml
SSH listener jail configuration.
.TP
.B ~/.adssh/recordings/
Session recordings.
.SH EXIT STATUS
With
.BR \-c ,
adssh exits with the status of the shell command, as
.B sh \-c
does
.RB ( "exit 3"
exits 3,
.B false
exits 1), and 1 on a Starlark, parse or policy error.
A script exits 0 on success and 1 on the first error.
The interactive shell exits with the status given to
.BI exit " n" ,
or with the status of the last command on a plain
.B exit
or Ctrl-D, as bash does; this holds when standard input is a pipe too.
An unknown option, or an option missing its value, exits 2.
A bad ADSSH_LOG_LEVEL or ADSSH_LOG_FORMAT exits 2.
Other startup failures exit 1.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run one shell command:
.PP
.nf
adssh \-c 'ls \-la | grep go'
.fi
.PP
Run one Starlark line:
.PP
.nf
adssh \-c 'print(sys.exec_cmd("uname \-s"))'
.fi
.PP
Force the shell for a line that would be Starlark:
.PP
.nf
! echo a=b
.fi
.PP
Capture command output in a variable at the prompt:
.PP
.nf
out = sys.exec_cmd("date")
.fi
.PP
Switch to vi keys:
.PP
.nf
set_keymap("vi")
.fi
.PP
Turn history suggestions off:
.PP
.nf
set suggest off
.fi
.PP
Create the starter configuration:
.PP
.nf
adssh \-\-init
.fi
.PP
Start a restricted login shell with a policy:
.PP
.nf
adssh \-\-login \-\-restricted \-\-policy /etc/adssh/policy.rego
.fi
.PP
Start the SSH server on port 2222:
.PP
.nf
adssh \-\-serve :2222
.fi
